# Files still reading a gated service-address config key as a literal, rather than resolving
# through Sorcha.ServiceClients.Configuration.SorchaServiceAddresses.
#
# RATCHET: may only shrink. A stale entry (file no longer carrying such a literal) fails the gate,
# so an entry must be removed in the same PR that removes the literal.
#
# THIS LIST IS EMPTY. Every service-address read in src/ now resolves through the resolver, so a
# new literal fails CI outright. Do not add an entry here to make a build pass — call
# SorchaServiceAddresses.TryResolve(configuration, SorchaService.X) and keep your own `?? default`
# (the resolver supplies none, deliberately — see the remarks on TryResolve: the existing defaults
# are not interchangeable, and unifying them is a separate decision).
#
# One key is intentionally NOT resolver-routed and is not a violation:
#   ServiceClients:PeerService:HttpAddress — a different endpoint, not a spelling variant. The Peer
#   service exposes gRPC on its resolved address and HTTP separately, so that key stays ahead of
#   the resolver at its two call sites in
#   src/Common/Sorcha.ServiceClients/Extensions/ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs, and is absent from
#   SorchaServiceAddresses.KeysFor(Peer) by design — folding it in would let a gRPC address answer
#   an HTTP lookup.
#
# The API Gateway is deliberately NOT gated at all: its `Services:{X}:Url` section is its own
# namespace for the aggregation views, is what compose sets for that container, and is already
# accepted by the resolver as a fallback spelling.
